================================================================================
DERIVED AND VENDORED WORK
================================================================================

kimi-k3-in-c (https://github.com/FareedKhan-dev/kimi-k3-in-c, Apache-2.0,
commit 81bb6c23ccee2154bc35cad922588ab66494a9a9, Copyright 2026 the kimi-k3-in-c
authors). This project reuses its technique (weights streamed from disk, MXFP4 experts
never dequantised, a budgeted expert cache) and the following files:

  third_party/json.h               minimal JSON parser
  third_party/tok.h                byte-level BPE tokenizer
  third_party/tok_unicode.h        Unicode character-class tables (cl100k)
  third_party/tok_unicode_o200k.h  Unicode character-class tables (o200k)

  These four were copied from that commit. They were themselves vendored and modified by
  the kimi-k3-in-c authors; that project's NOTICE lists those changes (the `kimi`
  pre-tokenizer flag and the `rankbpe` flag in tok.h, translated comments).

  MODIFICATIONS IN THIS PROJECT (Apache-2.0 section 4(b)):

    tok.h    Added a `qwen35` pre-tokenizer family (one \p{N} per piece, letters and
             marks as [\p{L}\p{M}], marks excluded from the punctuation rule), detected
             from the tokenizer.json pre_tokenizer regex; a `keep_merges` flag honouring
             `ignore_merges: false`; merges given as "left right" strings as well as
             two-element arrays; and tok_free(). With qwen35 unset, behaviour is
             unchanged.

    json.h   json_parse, json_get and json_free_tree declared `static inline`, so a file
             that includes the header without using all three compiles warning-free.
             No functional change.

  third_party/tok_unicode_nfc.h is generated by tests/fixtures/tok/gen_nfc.py from the
  Unicode Character Database 15.1 (via Python's unicodedata), used under the Unicode
  License v3.

  Files under src/ that are ported from kimi-k3-in-c say so in their header comment,
  naming the source file and the changes made (Apache-2.0 section 4(b)): the safetensors
  reader, portable I/O macros, memory probe, bf16 helpers, matvec kernels, RMSNorm,
  causal conv and delta-rule step (rewritten from k3_kda_step), and the sampler.

stb_image (https://github.com/nothings/stb, public domain / MIT, stb_image.h):
  vendored as third_party/stb_image.h. Used only for PNG and JPEG decode in
  src/io/lm_image.c (STBI_ONLY_JPEG, STBI_ONLY_PNG, STBI_NO_STDIO).

  MODIFICATIONS IN THIS PROJECT:

    stb_image.h  Cast w2*h2 and x*img_n multiplies to size_t before the product
                 (JPEG progressive coeff memset; PNG depth-8 memcpy size and 16-bit sample counts) so the
                 widen-after-multiply form CodeQL flags cannot truncate.

Hugging Face transformers (https://github.com/huggingface/transformers, Apache-2.0,
Copyright The HuggingFace Team and the Qwen team). The Python oracles under tools/ are
dependency-free ports of src/transformers/models/qwen3_5/modeling_qwen3_5.py (and, in
phase 2, the Gemma 4 modeling file), used only as test oracles.

================================================================================
MODEL WEIGHTS
================================================================================

This repository contains no model weights. Qwen3.5-4B (https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen3.5-4B)
and Gemma 4 E4B (https://huggingface.co/google/gemma-4-E4B-it) are released by their
authors under the Apache License 2.0; obtain them from the official distributions.
WhiteRabbitNeo-2.5-Qwen-2.5-Coder-7B (https://huggingface.co/WhiteRabbitNeo/WhiteRabbitNeo-2.5-Qwen-2.5-Coder-7B,
a fine-tune of Qwen/Qwen2.5-Coder-7B) is Apache-2.0 as well, as are bartowski's GGUF
quantisations of it (https://huggingface.co/bartowski/WhiteRabbitNeo-2.5-Qwen-2.5-Coder-7B-GGUF,
revision 4874da7ad5fa4da4f2584ae64ddc21f5a8e7dfce; the file's own metadata says
general.license = apache-2.0). The
Gemma 4 E4B Uncensored GGUF files used by the web picker's `gemma-uncensored` target
(https://huggingface.co/HauhauCS/Gemma-4-E4B-Uncensored-HauhauCS-Aggressive, a fine-tune of
google/gemma-4-e4b-it, revision 45b6a334b4bcd1d7f37179df58b3b1d66a184e5d) are tagged
`license: gemma` on their page: read the terms there before redistributing them. They are
downloaded by scripts/download-model.sh, never stored here, and run on llama.cpp's llama-server.
The
files under tests/fixtures/ are synthetic random tensors generated by tools/.
